<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1866"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Devbost<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Devbost<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 14, 2004 - 2:08 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Greetings everyone, <BR> <BR>I have been following the national media reports about BTK&#39;s re-emergence since his first effort to again make contact earlier this year, and really formed an interest after reading the recent Newsweek piece. <BR> <BR>Like many of you, I think the fact that we have audio of BTK&#39;s voice is extremely tantalizing. More so than his letters will provide, it&#39;s a scrap of his person that we can latch onto for some clue as to his identity and his psyche. Most serial killers don&#39;t leave a piece of personally identifying information like this that may prove useful for the police to investigate. <BR> <BR>Most serial killers seem like ordinary people once they&#39;re caught and we see news photos of them in court or in prison. No matter what they&#39;re guilty of, they don&#39;t project much difference from the rest of us. Even Jeffrey Dahmer looked like a regular guy, the orange prison suit notwithstanding. <BR> <BR>We hear BTK&#39;s voice on the audio captured after the Nancy Fox murder, and we know he&#39;s still out there, and in the absence of some photo of him in court or in the back of a police cruiser, in our imagination, he&#39;s still a terrifying phantom. It&#39;s creepy. <BR> <BR>Unfortunately, the audio we do have is of rather poor quality. The equipment that was used in 1977 to record phone calls obviously was not able to produce audio as clear as the equipment in use today. And BTK cleverly opted to place the call from a downtown phone booth. As anyone who has listened to the audio knows, it&#39;s tough to hear any of the voices of BTK or the two dispatchers with any clarity. At one point, the sound of what I think is a truck shifting gears overlaps BTK speaking. <BR> <BR>In an effort to get a clearer sound out of the audio, I began playing around with the graphic equalizer in Windows Media. The idea was to try and minimize the background noise while boosting the clarity of the voices. <BR> <BR>Using the audio file that Tom has made available from the front page of the site, I&#39;ve come up with a custom equalizer setting that I think provides a little better quality audio with regard to the voices. It&#39;s tinnier than the audio you&#39;ll hear with the default settings on, but it does boost the voice levels and helps a little bit to negate the ambient background noise. <BR> <BR>You can try it yourselves and see what you all think. I don&#39;t think that this provides us with something that can be of any practical use if you&#39;re talking about a professional voice analysis, but this is one of the best clues we have as to who BTK is that it would be foolish not to try and work with it as much as we can. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m using Windows Media Player 10. The graphic equalizer settings that I&#39;ve come up with are as follows, from left to right on the panel: <BR> <BR>31Hz: 9 <BR>62Hz: -4 <BR>125Hz: -14 <BR>250Hz: -14 <BR>500Hz: -4 <BR>1KHz: 14 <BR>2KHz: 10 <BR>4KHZ: -14 <BR>8KHz: -6 <BR>16KHz: 7 <BR> <BR>I also tried listening to it with all of the presets. These all sound much closer to the original audio than my settings do, but a couple of them yielded a clearer listen than the default settings. Of them, my impression was that the &#34;Grunge&#34; setting &#40;how appropriate, huh?&#41; provided the best quality without straying too far from the original. <BR> <BR>When it comes to tweaking audio, I&#39;m an educated amateur. I&#39;m sure that someone out there can improve on what I&#39;ve come up with. If anyone does, I&#39;d be very interested to hear the results.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
